#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""xui-split - light traffic over a mux'ed outbound, heavy traffic over a plain one,
separately for every exit the hub sends users to.

    xui-split                         test only: the groups found and what apply would build
    xui-split apply                   backup, test, build every enabled group, restart x-ui
    xui-split remove                  undo every group (keeps probe ports + RoutingService)
    xui-split groups [--json]         the groups, their inbounds, settings and state
    xui-split set GROUP [--on|--off] [--concurrency N] [--xudp N] [--label TEXT] [--heavy d1,d2]
                      [--auto]        one group's own settings (then apply, or fix for mux-only);
                                      --concurrency locks it there, --auto lets tune choose again
    xui-split tune [GROUP] [--apply] [--json]
                                      measure which mux concurrency serves each exit best and,
                                      with --apply, switch to it live (xui-split-tune.timer)
    xui-split check [--json]          is every group really working? outbound, mux, rules, probe, live Xray
    xui-split fix [--auto] [--json]   repair what check found: through Xray's API when only a mux
                                      outbound is wrong (no restart), else a full rebuild (restart);
                                      --auto only does the no-restart repairs (config-guard runs it)
    options: --no-tg-backup           leave the panel's Telegram backup setting alone

A group is one exit: an outbound that a catch-all rule sends users to (for example MAIN for
the server in one country, BACKUP for the one in another). Every group gets its own:

    outbound   <OUT>-mux                  a copy of <OUT> with mux on, its own concurrency
    rules      split-<out>-udp/heavy/telegram -> <OUT>, split-<out>-light -> <OUT>-mux,
               placed right before the group's catch-all, which is tagged main-<out>
    probe      guard-<out>-mux            so tunnel-guard watches each mux on its own
    settings   /etc/xui-split.json        on/off, concurrency, xudp, label, extra heavy domains

Outbounds that are only other routes to the same server (a second tunnel, a sing-box route,
a Reality path) are not groups: nothing routes users to them until tunnel-guard fails over.

Light traffic (web pages, messengers, APIs) rides an existing tunnel connection instead of
paying a TCP handshake across the link. Heavy traffic (video, downloads, updates), UDP and
Telegram stay without mux, so one big download never shares a connection with anyone's chat.

Smart mux (tune): how many streams should share one mux connection is not a constant. More
streams per connection save TCP+TLS handshakes across the tunnel (each costs a round trip of
80-100 ms or more); but one lost packet stalls every stream on that connection, so on a lossy
tunnel fewer are better. tune measures it on the exit's own light-traffic outbound in a
throwaway Xray (users are not touched): for concurrency 2, 4, 8, 16 and the current value it
opens 16 small HTTPS requests at once, like a page does, over several shuffled rounds after a
warm-up, and scores the median time with failures as a heavy penalty. Settings within 5% of the
best count as equal, and then the current one (or else the lowest) wins; with 2% or more packet
loss on the tunnel nothing above 4 is chosen. It switches only for a 25% gain, or a 10% gain
seen twice in a row, and applies through Xray's API (the mixed tunnels' copies too) with no
restart. An exit whose concurrency was set by hand is locked and left alone.

Also, for tunnel-guard: Xray's RoutingService is enabled so routes can be switched without a
restart, and every proxy outbound gets a local-only probe port (socks, 127.0.0.1:10808, ...).
The DNS cache's upstream rule is tagged "dns-upstream".
"""
